e_params itself does param.split('=') and raises ValueError first? Then the raise at 167 still happens (the ValueError propagates from _parse_params? No \u2014 _parse_params is called OUTSIDE the try block at line 152! `params = _parse_params(term, paramvals)` is before `try:`. So a ValueError inside _parse_params would NOT be caught at 167; it would propagate as raw ValueError. So for 167 to be hit, _parse_params must return params intact and the ValueError must occur in the loop. So _parse_params must NOT split on '='. I'm fairly confident _parse_params just splits on spaces (maybe with shlex). Let me verify with get_info.\n\nOK, making the calls now. I'll call:\n- get_info('LookupModule')\n- get_info('_parse_params')\n- get_info('LookupModule.get_value')\n- get_info('LookupBase')\n\nAll independent \u2192 same block.\n\nAfter that, write the final test script.\n\nLet me pre-draft the script:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nfrom ansible.errors import AnsibleLookupError, AnsibleOptionsError\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup import ini as ini_lookup\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup.ini import LookupModule\n\n\nBASE_OPTIONS = {\n    'file': 'test.ini',\n    'allow_no_value': False,\n    'allow_none': False,\n    'section': 'section1',\n    'key': None,\n    'default': None,\n    'case_sensitive': False,\n    'encoding': 'utf-8',\n    're': False,\n    'type': 'ini',\n}\n\n\ndef make_lookup(contents=b'', overrides=None):\n    \"\"\"Build a LookupModule with deterministic, isolated collaborators.\"\"\"\n    lookup = LookupModule()\n\n    options = dict(BASE_OPTIONS)\n    if overrides:\n        options.update(overrides)\n\n    lookup.set_options = lambda var_options=None, direct=None: None\n    lookup.get_options = lambda: dict(options)\n\n    loader = mock.MagicMock(name='loader')\n    loader._get_file_contents.return_value = (contents, False)\n    lookup._loader = loader\n\n    lookup.find_file_in_search_path = mock.MagicMock(return_value='/fake/test.ini')\n\n    return lookup\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\ndef silence_display(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Keep deprecation warnings from leaking into other tests.\"\"\"\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ini_lookup, 'display', mock.MagicMock())\n```\n\nWait \u2014 does ini.py have a module-level `display`? Need to confirm. If it's `from ansible.utils.display import display`, then ini_lookup.display exists. If it's `display = Display()`, also exists. If neither... then patching fails. Let me check with get_info \u2014 maybe get_info('LookupModule') shows the whole module source including imports. Or I can just guard: only patch if hasattr. Hmm, better to know. I'll check via get_info output.\n\nActually, alternatively, patch `ansible.plugins.lookup.ini.display` only if it exists:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\ndef silence_display(monkeypatch):\n    if hasattr(ini_lookup, 'display'):\n        monkeypatch.setattr(ini_lookup, 'display', mock.MagicMock())\n```\n\nThat's safe. But if display is imported as `from ansible.utils.display import display`, hasattr is True. OK.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe I shouldn't mock display at all \u2014 the real display.deprecated just prints a warning to stderr. The risk is only if expired deprecations raise. Let me think again about ansible-core versions:\n\nIn ansible-core 2.11-2.18, `Display.deprecated`:\n\n```python\n    def deprecated(self, msg, version=None, removed=False, date=None, collection_name=None):\n        if version is not None and not isinstance(version, tuple):\n            version = Version(version)\n        ...\n        if removed:\n            self.error(...)\n        else:\n            self.warning(...)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I now recall there IS this in newer versions:\n\n```python\n        if version is not None:\n            msg += \". This feature will be removed in version %s.\" % version\n```\n\nNo raising. But in ansible-core 2.19, there's `ansible.utils.display._deprecation`... I believe 2.19's Display.deprecated:\n\n```python\n    def deprecated(self, msg: str, version: str | None = None, removed: bool = False, date: str | None = None, collection_name: str | None = None) -> None:\n        ...\n        if removed:\n            self.error(message, wrap_text=False)\n        else:\n            self.warning(message, wrap_text=False)\n```\n\nStill no raise. OK, but there's also the `DEPRECATION_WARNINGS` config \u2014 if set to False, warnings suppressed. Default True \u2192 warning printed. That's just noise, not failure.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 there IS a mechanism where expired deprecations become errors: `ansible.errors.AnsibleError` raised in `Display.deprecated` when `removed=True`. And in `_deprecate_inline_kv`, removed is not set. So warning only. \n\nBut actually, hold on, I now genuinely remember that in ansible-core 2.19, Display.deprecated raises AnsibleError if the deprecation date/version has passed:\n\nFrom 2.19 display.py:\n\n```python\n    def deprecated(self, msg: str, version: str | None = None, removed: bool = False, date: str | None = None, collection_name: str | None = None) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Display a deprecation warning message.\n        ...\n        \"\"\"\n        message = _create_deprecation_message(...)\n        if removed:\n            self.error(message, wrap_text=False)\n        else:\n            self.warning(message, wrap_text=False)\n```\n\nHmm no. There's `ansible.module_utils.common.collections`... I can't recall precisely. The safe move: monkeypatch display in the ini module. It's cheap insurance and also keeps test output clean. I'll do it with hasattr guard... actually no guard \u2014 if `display` isn't a module attribute, monkeypatch.setattr raises AttributeError and I'd know. But I can't iterate after seeing failure... well, I can reason from get_info output. Let me get the module source via get_info('LookupModule') \u2014 hopefully it shows the file path and maybe full source.\n\nHmm, the get_info tool \"Returns information about a symbol\" \u2014 for a class, probably the class source. It may not show module-level imports. I could try get_info('display')? That's ambiguous. Let me try get_info('ansible.plugins.lookup.ini') \u2014 might return module info. Worth a shot alongside the others.\n\nNow, writing the tests concretely:\n\n```python\ndef test_simple_lookup():\n    \"\"\"Plain key lookup: covers option handling, file read, parse and single-value return.\"\"\"\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=value1\\nkey2=value2\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['key1']) == ['value1']\n    lookup.find_file_in_search_path.assert_called_once_with(None, 'files', 'test.ini')\n    lookup._loader._get_file_contents.assert_called_once_with('/fake/test.ini')\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_multiple_terms_and_values():\n    \"\"\"Two terms over one file; also covers the multi-value (MutableSequence) return path via regex.\"\"\"\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, let me keep regex separate.\n\n```python\ndef test_case_sensitive_lookup():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[Sect]\\nKey=Value\\n', overrides={'case_sensitive': True, 'section': 'Sect'})\n    assert lookup.run(['Key']) == ['Value']\n    # lowercase key must NOT match when case sensitive\n    lookup2 = make_lookup(b'[Sect]\\nKey=Value\\n', overrides={'case_sensitive': True, 'section': 'Sect'})\n    assert lookup2.run(['key']) == []\n```\n\nWait \u2014 with case_sensitive=True and key 'key' not found and default None \u2192 get_value returns None \u2192 ret []. Good, covers 198 False branch too.\n\nHmm, but careful: does get_value raise NoOptionError instead of returning None? Need real get_value source. If it does `self.cp.get(section, key)` in try/except NoOptionError \u2192 returns None. Let me confirm via get_info.\n\n```python\ndef test_case_insensitive_lookup():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nKey=Value\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['KEY']) == ['Value']\n```\n\nThis covers 142 False branch meaningfully (optionxform lowercases).\n\n