      lookup.run(['key1'])\n```\n\nCovers 196-197.\n\n```python\ndef test_regex_lookup_multiple_matches():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=a\\nkey2=b\\nother=c\\n', overrides={'re': True})\n    assert sorted(lookup.run(['key'])) == ['a', 'b']\n```\n\nCovers 199-201 (MutableSequence branch). Depends on get_value returning list for re=True. Need to verify.\n\n```python\ndef test_key_not_found_no_default():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=value1\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['missing']) == []\n```\n\nCovers 198 False (var None). Depends on get_value returning None.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, does get_value return None or raise NoOptionError? If it raises NoOptionError, run() doesn't catch it (only NoSectionError) \u2192 test fails. Need real get_value. From my memory of ini.py:\n\n```python\n    def get_value(self, key, section, dvalue, regular_expression):\n\n        # Retrieve file value\n        value = None\n        if regular_expression is True:\n            # regex search\n            matched = []\n            comp = re.compile(key)\n            for opt in self.cp.options(section):\n                if comp.match(opt):\n                    matched.append(self.cp.get(section, opt))\n            if len(matched) == 0 and dvalue is not None:\n                matched.append(dvalue)\n            value = matched\n        else:\n            if section != 'DEFAULT' and not self.cp.has_section(section):\n                raise configparser.NoSectionError(section)\n\n            if self.cp.has_option(section, key):\n                value = self.cp.get(section, key)\n            elif dvalue is not None:\n                value = dvalue\n\n        return value\n```\n\nHmm, I think there's something with `allow_no_value` handling:\n\n```python\n        else:\n            if section != 'DEFAULT' and not self.cp.has_section(section):\n                raise configparser.NoSectionError(section)\n\n            try:\n                if self.cp.getboolean(section, key)...\n```\n\nNo. Let me just get it.\n\nAlso \u2014 the regex branch: `self.cp.options(section)` \u2014 for section 'section1'. comp.match(opt) \u2014 'key' matches 'key1' and 'key2'. Returns ['a','b'] (order per cp.options \u2014 insertion order). Assert as set/sorted.\n\nWait, one more thing about regex: `if len(matched) == 0 and dvalue is not None: matched.append(dvalue)`. With default None and no match \u2192 value = [] (empty list, not None) \u2192 198 True, 199 True, loop over empty \u2192 nothing appended. Fine.\n\nAlso the default value test:\n\n```python\ndef test_default_value():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=value1\\n', overrides={'default': 'fallback'})\n    assert lookup.run(['missing']) == ['fallback']\n```\n\nCovers the dvalue path in get_value (not required but good).\n\nNow \u2014 the `allow_no_value` line 141: `paramvals.get('allow_no_value', paramvals.get('allow_none'))`. With our dict containing allow_no_value=False \u2192 ConfigParser(allow_no_value=False). To also exercise the fallback to allow_none... that's just an expression on line 141, covered anyway. Could add a test with allow_no_value absent? Our BASE_OPTIONS always has it. Eh \u2014 line 141 is covered regardless. But for branch coverage of the `.get` default... dict.get with default isn't a branch. Fine.\n\nHmm, what about `allow_no_value=True` behavior \u2014 a key with no value: \"[section1]\\nkey1\\n\" with allow_no_value=True \u2192 cp.get returns None \u2192 var None \u2192 ret []. That's a nice test for 198 False too. But depends on get_value: `if self.cp.has_option(section, key): value = self.cp.get(section, key)` \u2192 None. Then `elif dvalue...` no. Return None. OK maybe include. Actually, let me not over-engineer; the missing-key test covers 198 False.\n\nNow, about mocking `find_file_in_search_path`: I assign a MagicMock to the instance attribute. But wait \u2014 `run` calls `self.find_file_in_search_path(variables, 'files', paramvals['file'])`. With instance attribute MagicMock, works. \n\nBut hmm \u2014 should I instead let the real find_file_in_search_path run with a real loader? It calls `self._templar`... LookupBase.__init__ sets self._templar = templar (None if not passed). find_file_in_search_path:\n\n```python\n    def find_file_in_search_path(self, variables, subdir, needle, ignore_missing=False):\n        ''' \n        ...\n        '''\n        self._templar.set_available_variables(variables)\n        return self._find_needle(subdir, needle)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I think it's:\n\n```python\n    def find_file_in_search_path(self, variables, subdir, needle, ignore_missing=False):\n        self._templar.available_variables = variables\n        return self._find_needle('files', needle)\n```\n\nWith templar None \u2192 AttributeError. So must mock (or provide a Templar). Mocking is right.\n\nAlternatively \u2014 provide a real Templar with a fake variables dict... overkill. Mock.\n\nNow, `_loader._get_file_contents(path)` mocked to return (contents, show_data). Good.\n\nOne more: `to_text(contents, errors='surrogate_or_strict', encoding=paramvals['encoding'])` \u2014 contents bytes b'...' \u2192 decoded with utf-8. Good.\n\nNow let me also double check `run(['key1'])` \u2014 term 'key1' has no '=' and no space \u2192 150 False \u2192 straight to file lookup. Good.\n\nEdge: `' ' in term.strip()` \u2014 term 'key1' \u2192 no space. Good.\n\nNow the display patching. Let me write the fixture:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(autouse=True)\ndef _quiet_display(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(ini_lookup, 'display', mock.MagicMock(), raising=False)\n```\n\n`raising=False` avoids AttributeError if 'display' isn't there. Good \u2014 safe either way.\n\nHmm, but wait: if ini.py does `from ansible.utils.display import Display` and `display = Display()` at module level, then ini_lookup.display exists. If it imports `display` differently... raising=False handles it.\n\nActually, hmm, one concern: if `display` is NOT a module attribute and `_deprecate_inline_kv` references a global `display` that doesn't exist... then the real code would fail. But the real code works in production, so display must be accessible. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 is there any issue with `LookupModule()` constructor? LookupBase.__init__:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, loader=None, templar=None, **kwargs):\n        super(LookupBase, self).__init__()\n        self._loader = loader\n        self._templar = templar\n        self._basedir = ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\nclass LookupBase(AnsiblePlugin):\n\n    def __init__(self, loader=None, templar=None, **kwargs):\n\n        super(LookupBase, self).__init__()\n\n        self._loader = loader\n        self._templar = templar\n\n        # note: 'basedir' is ambiguous\n        self._cur_basedir = ...\n```\n\nI recall:\n\n```python\n        # cache, used for the 'basedir' of the current task\n        self._basedir = C.DEFAULT_LOCAL_TMP? \n```\n\nNo... Let me recall LookupBase:\n\n```python\nclass LookupBase(AnsiblePlugin):\n\n    def __init__(self, loader=None, templar=None, **kwargs):\n\n        super(LookupBase, self).__init__()\n\n        self._loader = loader\n        self._templar = templar\n        self._basedir = ...\n\n    def get_basedir(self, variables):\n        ...\n```\n\nI think there's `self._basedir = os.getcwd()`? Hmm:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, loader=None, templar=None, **kwargs):\n\n        super(LookupBase, self).__init__()\n\n        self._loader = loader\n        self._templar = templar\n\n        # default for 'in_process' worker\n        self._original_basedir = ...\n```\n\nWhatever \u2014 LookupModule() with no args is used in ansible's own unit tests (e.g., test/units/plugins/lookup/test_ini.py does `LookupModule()`? or with DataLoader). I'm fairly confident `LookupModule()` works standalone. In ansible's test_ini.py:\n\n```python\nclass TestIniLookup(unittest.TestCase):\n\n    def setUp(self):\n        self.lookup = Lo