h=\"Could not use\"):\n        lookup.run(['key1 file=a=b'])\n```\n\nCovers 167. The ValueError from 'file=a=b'.split('=') \u2192 3 values.\n\nWait \u2014 but 'file=a=b': name='file', value='a=b'? No: `name, value = param.split('=')` with param='file=a=b' \u2192 split gives ['file','a','b'] \u2192 ValueError \"too many values to unpack\". Yes \u2192 167.\n\nHmm, but hold on: does _parse_params maybe split 'file=a=b' itself? If _parse_params does `param.split('=')` it would raise ValueError inside _parse_params, which is OUTSIDE the try \u2192 propagates as ValueError, not AnsibleLookupError. Then my test fails. Need real _parse_params. If _parse_params uses shlex.split or .split() only, we're fine.\n\n```python\ndef test_inline_params_no_key():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=value1\\n')\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleOptionsError, match='No key to lookup'):\n        lookup.run(['file=test.ini'])\n```\n\nCovers 168-169. term 'file=test.ini' has '=' \u2192 inline path; params=['file=test.ini'] \u2192 all have '=' \u2192 updated_key False \u2192 raise.\n\nHmm wait \u2014 but what if _parse_params for a term with no space returns [term] or []? If term='file=test.ini' has no space, _parse_params might return [] or [term]. If [term], loop: '=' in param \u2192 paramvals['file'] set, updated_key False \u2192 raise. If [], loop doesn't run, updated_key False \u2192 raise. Either way 169 covered. But careful: if _parse_params returns [] and the for loop never runs, `param` in the except clause would be undefined \u2014 but no exception raised, so fine.\n\n```python\ndef test_duplicate_option_error():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=a\\nkey1=b\\n')\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match='Duplicate option'):\n        lookup.run(['key1'])\n```\n\nCovers 191-192.\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_section():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[other]\\nkey1=value1\\n')\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleLookupError, match=\"No section 'section1'\"):\n        lookup.run(['key1'])\n```\n\nCovers 196-197.\n\n```python\ndef test_regex_lookup_multiple_matches():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=a\\nkey2=b\\nother=c\\n', overrides={'re': True})\n    assert sorted(lookup.run(['key'])) == ['a', 'b']\n```\n\nCovers 199-201 (MutableSequence branch). Depends on get_value returning list for re=True. Need to verify.\n\n```python\ndef test_key_not_found_no_default():\n    lookup = make_lookup(b'[section1]\\nkey1=value1\\n')\n    assert lookup.run(['missing']) == []\n```\n\nCovers 198 False (var None). Depends on get_value returning None.\n\nHmm \u2014 wait, does get_value return None or raise NoOptionError? If it raises NoOptionError, run() doesn't catch it (only NoSectionError) \u2192 test fails. Need real get_value. From my memory of ini.py:\n\n```python\n    def get_value(self, key, section, dvalue, regular_expression):\n\n        # Retrieve file value\n        value = None\n        if regular_expression is True:\n            # regex search\n            matched = []\n            comp = re.compile(key)\n            for opt in self.cp.options(section):\n                if comp.match(opt):\n                    matched.append(self.cp.get(section, opt))\n            if len(matched) == 0 and dvalue is not None:\n                matched.append(dvalue)\n            value = matched\n        else:\n            if section != 'DEFAULT' and not self.cp.has_section(section):\n                raise configparser.NoSectionError(section)\n\n            if self.cp.has_option(section, key):\n                value = self.cp.get(section, key)\n            elif dvalue is not None:\n                value = dvalue\n\n        return value\n```\n\nHmm, I think there's something with `allow_no_value` handling:\n\n```python\n        else:\n            if section != 'DEFAULT' and not self.cp.has_section(section):\n                raise configparser.NoSectionError(section)\n\n            try:\n                if self.cp.getboolean(section, key)