out it more, is:\n\n```python\ndef _parse_params(term, paramvals):\n    ''' Splits the term into file and parameters (if any)'''\n    key = term\n\n    # parameters specified?\n    if ' ' in term:\n        # parse (split) out the params\n        params = shlex.split(term)\n        key = params[0]\n        params = params[1:]\n    else:\n        params = []\n\n    return params\n```\n\nHmm no wait. Actually! I remember now. The ini lookup's _parse_params:\n\n```python\ndef _parse_params(term, paramvals):\n    ''' Splits the term into file and parameters (if any)'''\n    key = term\n\n    # parameters specified?\n    if ' ' in term:\n        # parse (split) out the params\n        params = term.split()\n        # first is the key\n        key = params.pop(0)\n        ...\n```\n\nNo. OK here's the thing \u2014 I've seen this code. Let me reconstruct from the run() logic: run() does `key = term` then in the inline branch iterates params; `elif key == term:` sets key = param (first non-= param). This implies params includes ALL tokens (including the key token) in order. So _parse_params likely returns the full token list:\n\n```python\ndef _parse_params(term, paramvals):\n    ''' Splits the term into file and parameters (if any)'''\n    # parameters specified?\n    if ' ' in term:\n        return shlex.split(term)  # or term.split()\n    return [term]\n```\n\nHmm, or maybe it handles quoted strings. Something like:\n\n```python\ndef _parse_params(term, paramvals):\n    ''' Splits the term into file and parameters (if any)'''\n    rparams = []\n    # split on spaces, respecting quotes\n    ...\n```\n\nI'll see. The key question for my tests: does _parse_params preserve 'file=a=b' as a single token? If it uses shlex.split or .split(), yes \u2014 'file=a=b' has no spaces. Good.\n\nAnd does it raise on unknown params? If _parse_params itself validated names, the run() check at 158-159 would be dead code \u2014 but it's not dead (it's in the coverage target), so _parse_params